{"chunks": {"AAU_Groundnut_Disease_Management_Guide.pdf_p1_c0": {"chunk_id": "AAU_Groundnut_Disease_Management_Guide.pdf_p1_c0", "document_name": "AAU_Groundnut_Disease_Management_Guide.pdf", "page_number": 1, "chunk_index": 0, "text": "ANAND AGRICULTURAL UNIVERSITY (AAU) Department of Plant Pathology - Groundnut (■■■■■) Disease Advisory\n1. Tikka Leaf Spot (Cercospora) and Rust Management\nGroundnut Tikka disease produces circular dark brown to black spots with yellow halos on leaves, resulting in\npremature leaf shed. Management: At first appearance of spots (usually 35-40 days after sowing), spray Mancozeb\n75% WP @ 30-35 grams per 15-liter pump or Hexaconazole 5% EC @ 25-30 ml per 15-liter pump. Repeat after 15\ndays if cloudy humid weather persists. 2. Collar Rot and Stem Rot (Sclerotium rolfsii)\nCollar rot causes sudden seedling wilting with white mycelial growth and mustard-seed like sclerotia at the soil line.", "crops": ["Groundnut", "Mustard"], "symptoms": ["leaf_yellowing", "wilt_fungus"], "actions": ["pesticide"], "metadata": {"char_length": 689, "word_count": 107}}, "AAU_Groundnut_Disease_Management_Guide.pdf_p1_c1": {"chunk_id": "AAU_Groundnut_Disease_Management_Guide.pdf_p1_c1", "document_name": "AAU_Groundnut_Disease_Management_Guide.pdf", "page_number": 1, "chunk_index": 1, "text": "Bio-fungicide Treatment: Apply Trichoderma viride @ 50-60 grams per 15-liter pump around root zones or mix 2.5 kg\nTrichoderma with 500 kg well-decomposed farmyard manure (FYM) per acre prior to sowing. Avoid excessive\nirrigation that stagnates water around collars.", "crops": [], "symptoms": [], "actions": ["fertilizer", "irrigation"], "metadata": {"char_length": 265, "word_count": 39}}, "ICAR_CICR_Cotton_Crop_Advisory.pdf_p1_c0": {"chunk_id": "ICAR_CICR_Cotton_Crop_Advisory.pdf_p1_c0", "document_name": "ICAR_CICR_Cotton_Crop_Advisory.pdf", "page_number": 1, "chunk_index": 0, "text": "ICAR - CENTRAL INSTITUTE FOR COTTON RESEARCH (CICR) National Agricultural Advisory Bulletin: Comprehensive Cotton Health & Pest Management\n1. Cotton Leaf Yellowing and Chlorosis Management\nLeaf yellowing (chlorosis) in cotton is primarily caused by micronutrient deficiency, specifically Magnesium deficiency\nor Nitrogen deficiency, often exacerbated by waterlogging. When veins remain green while interveinal areas turn pale\nyellow or reddish (known locally as Lalio in Gujarat), foliar nutrition is urgent. Recommended Advisory: Spray\nMagnesium Sulphate (MgSO4) @ 10 grams per liter of water (150 grams per 15-liter pump) along with Urea @ 10-15\ngrams per liter. Apply 2 sprays at 15-day intervals during flowering and boll development. 2.", "crops": ["Cotton"], "symptoms": ["leaf_yellowing", "pest_infestation"], "actions": ["fertilizer", "pesticide", "irrigation"], "metadata": {"char_length": 741, "word_count": 106}}, "ICAR_CICR_Cotton_Crop_Advisory.pdf_p1_c1": {"chunk_id": "ICAR_CICR_Cotton_Crop_Advisory.pdf_p1_c1", "document_name": "ICAR_CICR_Cotton_Crop_Advisory.pdf", "page_number": 1, "chunk_index": 1, "text": "Apply 2 sprays at 15-day intervals during flowering and boll development. 2. Pink Bollworm (Pectinophora gossypiella) Control Strategy\nPink bollworm is a destructive pest causing rosetted flowers and locule damage. Monitoring must begin 45 days after\nsowing. Install pheromone traps @ 5 traps per acre with Gossyplure lures. If trap catches exceed 8 moths per night\nfor 3 consecutive days or 10% rosette flowers are observed, initiate chemical intervention. Recommended Spray:\nEmamectin Benzoate 5% SG @ 9-10 grams per 15-liter spray pump (200 g/ha) OR Chlorantraniliprole 18.5% SC @\n6-7 ml per 15-liter spray pump. Avoid continuous use of synthetic pyrethroids to prevent pest resistance. 3.", "crops": [], "symptoms": ["pest_infestation", "pink_bollworm"], "actions": ["pesticide"], "metadata": {"char_length": 692, "word_count": 106}}, "ICAR_CICR_Cotton_Crop_Advisory.pdf_p1_c2": {"chunk_id": "ICAR_CICR_Cotton_Crop_Advisory.pdf_p1_c2", "document_name": "ICAR_CICR_Cotton_Crop_Advisory.pdf", "page_number": 1, "chunk_index": 2, "text": "Avoid continuous use of synthetic pyrethroids to prevent pest resistance. 3. Sucking Pests (Aphids, Jassids, and Thrips)\nSucking pests infest the undersides of cotton leaves, draining sap and causing downward leaf curling, yellowing, and\nstunted growth. Bio-control Action: Spray commercial Neem Seed Kernel Extract (NSKE 5%) or Neem Oil (1500\nPPM) @ 45-50 ml per 15-liter spray pump. Chemical Intervention: If pest population exceeds Economic Threshold\nLevel (ETL) of 5 nymphs per leaf, spray Imidacloprid 17.8% SL @ 4-5 ml per 15-liter spray pump or Thiamethoxam\n25% WG @ 6-8 grams per 15-liter pump. 4. Agro-Chemical Safety and Personal Protective Equipment (PPE)\nFarmer Safety Protocol: Always wear personal protective equipment including face mask, safety goggles, rubber\ngloves, and protective clothing.", "crops": ["Cotton"], "symptoms": ["leaf_yellowing", "pest_infestation", "leaf_curl", "aphids_whitefly"], "actions": ["pesticide"], "metadata": {"char_length": 809, "word_count": 120}}, "ICAR_CICR_Cotton_Crop_Advisory.pdf_p1_c3": {"chunk_id": "ICAR_CICR_Cotton_Crop_Advisory.pdf_p1_c3", "document_name": "ICAR_CICR_Cotton_Crop_Advisory.pdf", "page_number": 1, "chunk_index": 3, "text": "Always spray in the direction of the wind; never spray against wind direction. Spray\nstrictly during cool hours (morning before 10 AM or evening after 4 PM). Strictly avoid banned compounds including\nEndosulfan and Monocrotophos. Maintain a mandatory 15-day pre-harvest interval (PHI).", "crops": [], "symptoms": [], "actions": ["pesticide"], "metadata": {"char_length": 285, "word_count": 42}}}, "vectors": {"AAU_Groundnut_Disease_Management_Guide.pdf_p1_c0": [0.021529752730239896, 0.0, -0.07490089987023604, 0.021529752730239896, 0.0, -0.04305950546047979, -0.021529752730239896, -0.01031164167951636, 0.04305950546047979, 0.021529752730239896, -0.021529752730239896, -0.03184139440975626, 0.0, 0.